 
From Gambling to Securities: Legal and Operational Blueprint for Prediction Markets in Georgia


Legal and Regulatory Framework for a Tokenized Event Trading Platform in Georgia
The development of a tokenized event trading platform in Georgia requires a careful legal and regulatory analysis to ensure that the platform is classified appropriately under Georgian law. The central question is whether such a platform qualifies as a gambling activity under the Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games or as a securities trading platform under the Law of Georgia on Securities Market. Under the gambling regime, activities are considered games of chance if participants risk money or economic value on outcomes that are partially or entirely determined by chance, with the opportunity to receive prizes, and enforcement and licensing authority is vested in the Revenue Service of Georgia (Revenue Service of Georgia, n.d.; Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games, Art. 2). Conversely, the securities regime governs tradeable financial instruments and market infrastructure, with the National Bank of Georgia (NBG) as the principal regulator (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 1).
For a tokenized event trading platform to avoid classification as gambling, the economic structure must ensure that gains or losses are derived from market-determined outcomes rather than chance-based events with prizes. According to Article 2 of the gambling law, a game of chance includes three key elements: stake, chance, and prize. In contrast, trading tokens that represent financial instruments and whose value fluctuates based on supply and demand dynamics does not constitute a “game of chance” because participants’ profits or losses are tied to the market performance of the instrument rather than a predefined prize (Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games, Art. 2; Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 1). Therefore, the platform’s design, in which the intermediary does not gain profit from user trades but earns revenue from fees or commissions, further supports its non-gambling classification.
A precedent for regulated financial markets in Georgia is the Tbilisi Stock Exchange (TSE), which operates under a license issued by NBG and facilitates the trading of securities in a regulated environment (Tbilisi Stock Exchange, n.d.). Modeled after this, a tokenized event trading platform can operate within the securities law framework, provided it issues tokens classified as tradeable financial instruments and follows all intermediary and investor protection regulations. This approach requires early engagement with NBG, preferably through the regulatory sandbox, which allows innovators to submit detailed business models, technical mechanisms, risk management frameworks, and compliance systems for preliminary assessment. Early engagement with NBG can clarify the legal classification and ensure that the platform avoids requalification under the gambling regime.
Regarding entity structure, the platform can operate as either a broker/dealer or a full exchange operator. A broker/dealer serves as an intermediary executing trades for users without hosting a full order book or providing clearing services, whereas an exchange operator provides a complete trading venue with full market infrastructure, central clearing, and settlement capabilities. For a tokenized event platform in the early stage, the broker/dealer model is recommended. This model offers several advantages: it reduces regulatory and capital requirements, allows faster licensing, provides operational flexibility, and aligns with sandbox experimentation (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 1). The full exchange operator model is more appropriate at later stages, when the platform has sufficient trading volumes, liquidity, and technical and financial capacity to satisfy NBG’s rigorous requirements, including operational transparency, risk management, and investor protection obligations.
The step-by-step procedure for legally operating a tokenized event trading platform in Georgia is as follows:
Legal Classification: Draft a memorandum demonstrating that the platform’s tokens are financial instruments, not gambling products, citing the gambling law (Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games, Art. 2) and the securities law (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 1).
Sandbox Application: Submit the platform’s business model, technical architecture, token issuance documentation, AML/KYC protocols, and risk management framework to the NBG Sandbox for preliminary assessment.
Entity Formation: Register a local legal entity via the National Agency of Public Registry and decide on the operational model — broker/dealer initially, with the potential to evolve into a full exchange operator.
Regulatory Documentation: Prepare comprehensive documentation including business plan, governance structure, risk management policies, cybersecurity protocols, AML/KYC procedures, and technical system descriptions.
License Application: Submit the broker/dealer license application to NBG, including all corporate and technical documentation. The NBG reviews and may request clarifications or supplementary information.
Compliance and Restrictions: Implement and maintain robust compliance procedures, including AML/KYC verification, investor protection measures, transparent fee structures, operational limits, and cybersecurity safeguards. Ensure no prize-based payouts occur and token trading is strictly market-driven.
Approval and Operation: Upon NBG approval, operate legally as a broker/dealer under securities law. Ensure ongoing reporting, risk monitoring, and readiness for audits.
Future Expansion: Consider transitioning to a full exchange operator to host multiple instruments, manage order books, and provide full clearing and settlement once the platform scales sufficiently. This mirrors the TSE operational model (Tbilisi Stock Exchange, n.d.).
In conclusion, a tokenized event trading platform can be legally and operationally structured in Georgia to operate under the securities law framework while avoiding gambling classification. Starting as a broker/dealer licensed under the Law of Georgia on Securities Market offers an optimal balance of regulatory compliance, operational flexibility, and market credibility. This approach ensures that the platform’s tokenized assets are treated as tradeable securities, aligns with investor protection standards, and provides a clear path for eventual evolution into a full exchange operator akin to the Tbilisi Stock Exchange.
Operational Model for a Prediction Market in Georgia
The proposed prediction market platform in Georgia is designed to facilitate trading of tokenized event outcomes, structured as financial instruments rather than gambling products. Each token represents a specific, measurable outcome, such as election results, sports statistics, or economic indicators. Participants can buy and sell these tokens based on their prediction of the event’s result, and the token’s market price reflects collective market expectations. Unlike gambling, the platform does not guarantee any fixed prize; users’ gains and losses depend entirely on the market valuation of the token, determined by supply, demand, and the actual event outcome. Therefore, the platform’s economic structure ensures it is classified under the Law of Georgia on Securities Market, avoiding the application of the Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 1; Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games, Art. 2).
The platform operates under a broker/dealer model, functioning as a financial intermediary. It facilitates the issuance, listing, and trading of tokenized event outcomes while charging fees or commissions for execution and settlement services. Importantly, the platform itself does not profit from the outcomes of the trades, ensuring it does not meet the “prize” element defined under Georgian gambling law (Law of Georgia on Organising Lotteries, Games of Chance and Other Prize Games, Art. 2). All trading activity is transparent and market-driven, and participants bear the economic risk and reward of their positions.
Trading Mechanics and Instruments
The primary instruments traded on the platform are tokenized event outcome tokens, each representing a binary or multi-outcome event. For example:
Binary Tokens – Represent a yes/no outcome for a specific event (e.g., “Candidate X will win the election”). 
Multi-Outcome Tokens – Represent multiple possible outcomes for a single event, each token priced based on market expectation. 
Fractionalized Tokens – Represent partial ownership of event outcomes or pooled positions. 
Trading is conducted via a centralized order book managed by the platform. Prices are determined by supply and demand dynamics, and all transactions are settled digitally. The platform may implement risk management measures such as position limits and margin requirements to ensure stability and protect participants. Participants must complete KYC (Know Your Customer) verification to comply with AML (Anti-Money Laundering) regulations, and the platform enforces investor protection measures, including full disclosure of risk and market volatility (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 12; National Bank of Georgia, n.d.).
Compliance and Regulatory Oversight
To operate legally, the platform must obtain a broker/dealer license from the National Bank of Georgia (NBG) and may initially participate in the NBG regulatory sandbox to ensure compliance with securities market requirements. Licensing documentation includes the corporate incorporation certificate, business plan, governance structure, technical infrastructure details, AML/KYC procedures, and risk management frameworks. Continuous compliance involves:
Transaction monitoring for fraud, market abuse, and insider trading. 
AML/KYC verification for all participants. 
Risk disclosure and investor education. 
Technical security measures to protect token custody and settlement. 
Unlike a gambling platform, the intermediary does not hold user funds for betting purposes; all economic value is held in the form of digital tokens representing market-traded positions, making the operation consistent with Georgian securities law (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 1).
Operational Workflow
Token Issuance: Event outcomes are defined and tokenized, with each token representing a measurable, verifiable outcome. 
Listing: Tokens are listed on the platform’s order book. 
Trading: Participants buy and sell tokens via the broker/dealer platform, with market prices reflecting collective expectation. 
Settlement: Upon event resolution, token positions are settled based on actual outcomes. 
Reporting: Continuous reporting to NBG ensures compliance with securities law. 
By structuring operations in this manner, the platform functions analogously to the Tbilisi Stock Exchange, which facilitates regulated securities trading while maintaining investor protections, market transparency, and operational integrity (Tbilisi Stock Exchange, n.d.). This ensures that the prediction market remains fully compliant under the securities framework and avoids classification as gambling.
Comprehensive Operational and Technical Framework for a Prediction Market Platform in Georgia
1. Technical Architecture
The prediction market platform will operate on a centralized broker/dealer model with modular components to ensure scalability, security, and regulatory compliance. The architecture consists of the following layers:
Frontend Layer: Web and mobile interfaces for participant registration, wallet management, trading, and reporting. Access is secured with two-factor authentication (2FA) and encrypted communications (TLS 1.3). 
Application Layer: Handles business logic including order matching, token pricing, user position management, event resolution, and transaction logging. This layer ensures that trades are executed in accordance with market rules and that all transactions are auditable. 
Blockchain / Ledger Layer: Digital tokens representing event outcomes are recorded on a private or permissioned blockchain to ensure immutability, transparency, and verifiability of token ownership. Smart contracts may automate settlement based on verified event outcomes. 
Data & Analytics Layer: Stores historical trading data, token prices, and user activity for regulatory reporting, market analysis, and risk monitoring. Data retention policies comply with NBG requirements for auditability and transparency. 
Infrastructure Layer: Servers are physically located in Georgia to comply with local regulations. This layer includes redundant storage, backup, disaster recovery, and 24/7 monitoring. Cybersecurity controls follow international standards (ISO/IEC 27001). 
This architecture ensures that the platform is resilient, secure, and capable of meeting NBG’s reporting and operational standards (National Bank of Georgia, n.d.).

2. Token Issuance Documentation
Each token represents a discrete, measurable event outcome. The issuance process includes:
Event Definition: Clearly define the event, its measurable outcomes, and resolution method. Examples include political elections, sports results, or macroeconomic indicators. 
Token Structure: Each token is divisible, fungible within its class, and linked to a specific event outcome. Tokens carry no guaranteed prize and derive value solely from market demand. 
Smart Contracts: Automated contracts encode issuance rules, transferability, and settlement logic. Upon event resolution, smart contracts execute token settlement and update participant balances automatically. 
Whitepaper / Disclosure: Comprehensive documentation including event definition, token mechanics, settlement process, risk factors, fee structure, and legal disclaimers is provided to all participants to satisfy investor protection standards under the Law on Securities Market (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 12). 
Audit Certification: Independent verification of smart contracts and token issuance ensures accuracy, compliance, and security, minimizing operational and regulatory risks. 

3. AML / KYC Protocols
To comply with Georgian anti-money laundering laws and NBG regulations, the platform implements a robust AML/KYC framework:
Customer Verification: Users must submit identification documents (passport, national ID) verified against government databases. Foreign participants are subject to equivalent international KYC standards. 
Risk Screening: All users are screened against PEP (Politically Exposed Persons) lists, sanction lists, and known high-risk entities. 
Transaction Monitoring: Continuous real-time monitoring detects unusual trading patterns or suspicious fund flows. Alerts trigger manual review and reporting to authorities if needed. 
Enhanced Due Diligence: For participants trading above a specified threshold (e.g., GEL 5,000 equivalent), enhanced checks verify source of funds and business intent. 
Record Keeping: All AML/KYC records are retained for a minimum of 10 years, enabling auditability and compliance with NBG reporting requirements. 
This ensures that the platform does not facilitate illegal or high-risk financial activity and adheres strictly to Georgian financial market standards (Law of Georgia on Securities Market, Art. 12).

4. Risk Management Framework
The platform’s risk management framework addresses operational, market, and compliance risks:
Market Risk: Implements position limits, margin requirements, and liquidity monitoring to prevent excessive exposure by participants. 
Operational Risk: Redundant servers, disaster recovery plans, and 24/7 monitoring minimize downtime or service disruptions. Smart contracts are independently audited to prevent code failures or exploits. 
Cybersecurity Risk: End-to-end encryption, DDoS protection, intrusion detection, and regular penetration testing ensure platform integrity. 
Legal & Compliance Risk: Continuous review of Georgian securities law and NBG regulations ensures ongoing compliance. Reporting and audit processes are standardized. 
Settlement Risk: Automated smart contract settlement reduces counterparty risk, ensuring trustless, verifiable execution of trades. 
User Risk Awareness: Participants are provided with full disclosure of risks, including the market-based nature of token pricing and potential loss of invested funds. 
This structured approach ensures that the prediction market operates safely, transparently, and in full compliance with NBG regulations.

5. Operational Workflow
Sandbox Engagement: Submit business plan, technical architecture, and compliance documentation to NBG’s regulatory sandbox for preliminary review. 
Token Issuance & Listing: Define events, issue tokens, and list them for trading. 
Participant Onboarding: Implement KYC/AML checks and risk disclosure. 
Trading & Settlement: Participants trade tokens via the platform; settlements are automated via smart contracts upon event resolution. 
Monitoring & Reporting: Continuous monitoring of market activity, compliance checks, and submission of regulatory reports to NBG. 
Scalability & Expansion: With growth, platform may evolve into a full exchange operator, offering broader instruments and clearing capabilities akin to the Tbilisi Stock Exchange (Tbilisi Stock Exchange, n.d.).

